Context (§ on 'triplets modulaires', ca. p. 332-335): S is a base scheme, G is a group scheme locally isomorphic to GL(2)_S, and PG is its adjoint form, locally isomorphic to PGL(2)_S (written GP(1)/GPl(1)). Tripmod(G) is the set of triples of sections (ρ,σ,ε0) of G satisfying conditions (52)-(56) of the previous page:
- -σ^{-1}ρ = ε0;
- ε0 is regular unipotent in every fibre;
- ε0 and ε1 := ρε0ρ^{-1} are 'inequivalent' in every fibre;
- σ²+1 = 0, equivalently ρ²-ρ+1 = 0 (Tr σ = 0, Tr ρ = det ρ = 1).

In other words, it is a homomorphism from SL2(Z) = <ρ,σ>, sent to elements of order 4 and 6 (elliptic generators) and a regular unipotent (the image of T^{-1}, related by the 'loop' relation), with fibrewise non-degeneracy conditions. The standard triple (57) is ρ0 = [[0,1],[-1,1]], σ0 = [[0,-1],[1,0]], ε0 = [[1,-1],[0,1]]. One checks that -σ0^{-1}ρ0 = ε0.

On this page, diagram (58) maps an isomorphism φ: GL(2)_S → G to (φ(ρ0), φ(σ0), φ(ε0)), and similarly ψ: PGL(2)_S → PG to the images of the standard projective triple. The vertical arrows pass to the adjoint group. The 'Théorème d'épinglage' asserts that all arrows are bijective. A modular triple would thus play the role of a pinning (épinglage, as in SGA3 / Chevalley-Demazure), replacing torus + root vectors by the images of the elliptic and parabolic generators of the modular group.

The page then unpacks injectivity of the horizontal arrows. An isomorphism (and in particular an automorphism) is determined by its values on σ and ρ, hence on ε0. For GL(2) this gives (59): the intersection of the centralizers of ρ and σ is the centre. For PGL(2) it gives the stronger (60): N'_ρ ∩ N'_σ = centre, where (next page) N'_x = {g | gxg^{-1} = εx, ε scalar}, so that ε² = 1.

The statement serves the 'équation des lacets' / Teichmüller-tower programme. It gives a rigidification of forms of GL2/PGL2 by the image of the modular group SL2(Z) = Z/4 *_{Z/2} Z/6. This leads on the next page to the question whether PGL(2)_Z (resp. GL(2)_Z, really SL(2)_Z) is, as an fpqc sheaf, the amalgam of Z/2, Z/3 and G_a with the single relation (62) (resp. Z/4, Z/6, G_a with (63)).
